<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    MikeOS ROM — framework config overlay.

    This is a RUNTIME/BUILD resource overlay against
    frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml. It is applied because
    common.mk adds `vendor/mikeos/overlay` to PRODUCT_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS, so at
    build time aapt merges these values on top of the framework defaults.

    Purpose: make MikeOS Home the default HOME role holder out of the box.
    LineageOS ships Trebuchet as its launcher; by overriding config_defaultHome
    the RoleManager assigns the HOME role to com.mikeos.launcher on first boot,
    so there is NO "pick a launcher" chooser dialog. Trebuchet stays installed
    (still selectable in Settings > Apps > Default apps) but is not default.

    KNOWN-UNVERIFIED (see README): the exact resource name/type. In AOSP this is
    a <string> named `config_defaultHome` holding a flattened ComponentName
    "pkg/cls". Verify against the lineage-23.2 tree:
        frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
    If Lineage/Google already sets a default-home config for tegu, this overlay
    value wins (overlays override, last-writer). If the framework instead uses
    a different mechanism on Android 16 (e.g. a role-config resource array),
    adjust the name/type here to match the base resource exactly — an overlay
    that names a non-existent resource is silently dropped by aapt.

    The launcher Activity is com.mikeos.launcher/com.mikeos.launcher.MainActivity
    — CONFIRM the real launcher-intent Activity class from the shipped
    com.mikeos.launcher APK manifest (the one with CATEGORY_HOME +
    CATEGORY_DEFAULT). If it differs, fix the class name below.
-->
<resources xmlns:xliff="urn:oasis:names:tc:xliff:document:1.2">

    <!-- The default home Activity the RoleManager grants the HOME role to. -->
    <string name="config_defaultHome" translatable="false"
        >com.mikeos.launcher/com.mikeos.launcher.MainActivity</string>

    <!--
        NAVIGATION MODE — gestural (iOS-style home indicator) as the BAKED default.

        0 = 3-button, 1 = 2-button, 2 = gestural  (AOSP config_navBarInteractionMode).

        HISTORY — READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT BACK TO 0. This was 0 on the premise that
        "MikeOS removes Launcher3QuickStep, so gestural navigation has NO handler and the
        user boots into a full-screen app with no way out". That premise is FALSE for the
        shipped ROM, and was already false when it was written:

          * PRODUCT_PACKAGES_REMOVE in config/common.mk lists only LineageSetupWizard and
            Provision — Launcher3QuickStep is NOT in it. (And PRODUCT_PACKAGES_REMOVE is a
            no-op against inherited packages anyway, so listing it would not have removed
            it either.)
          * On a v37 device Launcher3QuickStep ships baked at
            /system_ext/priv-app/Launcher3QuickStep/Launcher3QuickStep.apk and
            com.android.launcher3/com.android.quickstep.TouchInteractionService is
            RUNNING. The daemon launcher already says "QuickStep is back since v13" — the
            nav decision was simply never revisited after that.

        VERIFIED on tegu 62271JEBF09145 (Android 16) with mode 2 live: swipe-from-left-edge
        = Back and swipe-up-from-bottom = Home both work, driven by QuickStep. Gestural has
        a handler, so the "no way out" risk does not apply.

        Why gestural is the default now: the 3-button bar is a ~48dp opaque strip that sat
        ON TOP of app content and the launcher dock — that is what "the buttons hide things"
        meant. Gestural replaces it with a thin home indicator, the iOS model MikeOS Home
        targets. Apps additionally inset content into the safe area (each app's Compose
        theme wraps content in Modifier.safeDrawingPadding), so nothing hides under the
        indicator either. This also retires the old caveat that Recents had no Overview
        provider: QuickStep supplies Overview.

        The device's RRO is not force-enabled by a baked OverlayConfig, so this base value
        wins. The daemon launcher (vendor/mikeos/system/bin/mikeos-daemon) applies the
        MATCHING gestural overlay as belt-and-suspenders — keep the two in sync, or a
        reboot silently undoes this.
    -->
    <integer name="config_navBarInteractionMode">2</integer>

</resources>
